{"name":"Big Ocean Wave","hex":"#34708f","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"big","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Biangai."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of great size, large.","Fat."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/big"},{"term":"ocean","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["One of the large bodies of water separating the continents.","The continuous body of salt water covering a majority of the Earth's surface."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ocean"},{"term":"wave","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To move back and forth repeatedly and somewhat loosely.","To move one's hand back and forth (generally above the shoulders) in greeting or departure."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A moving disturbance in the level of a body of liquid; an undulation.","The ocean."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wave"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ocean Wave","blocks":[{"text":"Ocean Wave was an American Thoroughbred racehorse who was a main rival of\nCount Fleet in the early 1940s. Owned by Calumet Farm, he was trained by Ben Jones and owned by Warren Wright, Ocean Wave was the son of Blenheim\nand Columbiana. He developed into a come from behind threat as a two-year-old and a three-year-old."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ocean_Wave","wikidata":"Q7076116"},{"title":"Wind wave","blocks":[{"text":"In fluid dynamics, a wind wave, or wind-generated water wave, is a surface wave that occurs on the free surface of bodies of water as a result of the wind blowing over the water's surface. The contact distance in the direction of the wind is known as the fetch. Waves in the oceans can travel thousands of kilometers before reaching land. Wind waves on Earth range in size from small ripples to waves over 30 m (100 ft) high, being limited by wind speed, duration, fetch, and water depth."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Porto_Covo_Outubro_2014-3.jpg/330px-Porto_Covo_Outubro_2014-3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wind_wave","wikidata":"Q165848"},{"title":"Big Ocean","blocks":[{"text":"Big Ocean (Korean: 빅오션) is the first hard of hearing South Korean boy band, formed under Parastar Entertainment. The group consists of three members: Lee Chan-yeon, PJ, and Kim Ji-seok. They debuted on April 20, 2024, with the single \"Glow\", which coincided with the Day of Persons with Disabilities in South Korea. According to The New York Times, Big Ocean \"has raised awareness of Deaf culture in Korea and the world.\""}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Big_Ocean","wikidata":"Q125100381"},{"title":"Ocean","blocks":[{"text":"The ocean is the body of salt water that covers approximately 70.8% of Earth. The ocean is conventionally divided into large bodies of water, which are also referred to as oceans, and are themselves mostly divided into seas, gulfs and subsequent bodies of water. The ocean contains 97% of Earth's water and is the primary component of Earth's hydrosphere, acting as a huge reservoir of heat for Earth's energy budget, as well as for its carbon cycle and water cycle, forming the basis for climate and weather patterns worldwide. The ocean is essential to life on Earth, harbouring most of Earth's animals and protist life. It originated photosynthesis and therefore Earth's atmospheric oxygen, and still supplies half of it."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Pacific_Ocean_as_viewed_from_GOES-18_on_September_23%2C_2023.jpg/330px-Pacific_Ocean_as_viewed_from_GOES-18_on_September_23%2C_2023.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ocean","wikidata":"Q9430"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Sjyang_waveGeneration.png/500px-Sjyang_waveGeneration.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Sjyang_waveGeneration.png/1280px-Sjyang_waveGeneration.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Sjyang_waveGeneration.png/500px-Sjyang_waveGeneration.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Wave formation","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASjyang_waveGeneration.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Deep_water_wave.gif/330px-Deep_water_wave.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Deep_water_wave.gif/330px-Deep_water_wave.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Water particle motion of a deep water wave","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADeep_water_wave.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Ocean_wave_phases_numbered.png/500px-Ocean_wave_phases_numbered.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Ocean_wave_phases_numbered.png/1280px-Ocean_wave_phases_numbered.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Ocean_wave_phases_numbered.png/500px-Ocean_wave_phases_numbered.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The phases of an ocean surface wave: 1. Wave Crest, where the water masses of the surface layer are moving horizontally in the same direction as the propagating wavefront. 2. Falling wave. 3. Trough, where the water masses of the surface layer are moving horizontally in the opposite direction of the wavefront direction. 4. Rising wave.","link":"